.\" Template. The Makefile renders this into build/<command>.1 by substituting
.\" the @PLACEHOLDERS@ from src/core/branding.py — edit this file, never the
.\" generated one, and never write the project's name here.
.TH @COMMAND_UPPER@ 1 "@DATE@" "@NAME@ @VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
@COMMAND@ \- @DESCRIPTION@
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B @COMMAND@
.RI [ command ]
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B @NAME@
turns interview practice into timed, scored, permanently recorded runs.
You solve problems in a browser and report the verdict yourself;
.B @NAME@
times the attempt, records what happened, and prompts you to write down what
made it hard while you still remember.
.PP
Everything is stored as an append-only event log. Run summaries and rankings
are projections rebuilt from that log, and scores are computed when you read
them rather than stored, so changing the scoring weights re-scores all of your
history at once. Nothing is ever overwritten.
.PP
What is practised is not fixed. A
.I type
says what kind of thing a problem is \(em what is asked after a solve, which
screens follow, how the answers move a review, what par is \(em and a
.I set
is a list of problems that names the type they are. Both are files you write,
and every type is scheduled by the same cards. See
.BR "PROBLEM TYPES AND SETS" .
.PP
Speech mode, off by default, additionally records what you say while each
problem runs — one Opus file per attempt, pausing exactly when the clock does.
It needs
.B ffmpeg
on the path and nothing else; without one, a run says so and carries on
unrecorded. Turn it on in the settings screen, or with
.B ^a
on the setup screen for a single run. The active problem screen shows
.B "\(bu REC"
for as long as the microphone is open.
.PP
With no command,
.B @COMMAND@
starts the terminal interface and lands on the home screen.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B stats
Solve times as distributions rather than averages: the 50th, 90th and 99th
percentiles, what is dragging the tail, and how the current window compares to
the one before it. The 99th percentile is greyed out on small samples, where it
is one attempt and not a statistic.
.TP
.B history
Every run, ranked against every other run.
.TP
.B queue
Today's queue: which problems to do, in what order, and why. Reviews are
scheduled by FSRS from your own history and lead the queue, but take only
.I session.reviews_per_day
slots of it \(em one by default \(em so new coverage does not stall. When
several problems are due the slot goes to the one you have forgotten most
rather than the one that has waited longest, and the rest are deferred. The
remaining slots go to problems you have never opened, drawn from your weakest
patterns. No problem attempted in the last three days appears unless it has come
due, the difficulty mix is targeted at roughly 20/60/20, and no pattern ever runs
three problems deep: interleaving beats blocking for transfer. Generated on first
use each day.
.TP
.B mastered
Problems that have left the rotation. A problem is mastered once it has been
recalled across its whole ladder \(em once after an easy solve, up to four times
after a failed one \(em and stops being offered. It is hidden rather than
deleted: losing one in a mixed run puts it back on the failed ladder. A mastered
problem carries a star in front of its name wherever one is listed.
.TP
.B methods
Every way you know to solve a problem: the route, what it costs, whether it is
the optimal one, and whether you have written it. Name a problem for one list,
or omit it for all of them. Read-only \(em the screen on
.B a
inside the app is where these are edited. Which patterns a problem can be solved
with is a separate list, on
.B p
inside the app.
.TP
.B replay
Rebuild all projections from the event log. Safe to run at any time; it changes
no history, only the tables derived from it.
.TP
.B seed
Bring the problem catalog in line with the configuration: every set named under
.I [sets]
is read and its problems inserted or updated, each stamped with the set's type.
Prints how many problems each set held and any entry it left out. The same sync
runs every time the app opens, so this is for seeing what it did. Never touches
attempt history.
.TP
.B types
The problem types: what each one asks after a solve, which screens follow, how
each answer moves a review, and whether the type came from the package or from
your configuration directory. Name one for that type alone. A type is what makes
a set of system design prompts ask how many requirements you covered where a set
of LeetCode problems asks what the solution costs; both are scheduled by the
same cards.
.TP
.B fetch
Cache problem statements for offline use, so a flight is a normal run. Covers
every list whose type has somewhere to fetch from, the active list first, and
downloads the whole of each in priority order — today's queue first,
then cards by due date, then the rest spread across patterns — until
.I cache.max_mb
is spent. Self-contained pages with images inlined; nothing is left to request.
Idempotent and resumable, and a problem that will not download is reported
rather than ending the sweep. Set
.I cache.offline
to point
.B o
at the cached copy instead of the browser. Inside the app this is
.B warm the cache
on the settings screen, one row under that switch.
.TP
.B doctor
Print the resolved paths, catalog size, editor, cache state and configuration.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS stats
.TP
.BI \-\-tag " TAG"
Restrict to problems carrying a tag.
.TP
.BI \-\-pattern " PATTERN"
Restrict to one pattern group, e.g.
.IR sliding-window .
.TP
.BI \-\-difficulty " easy|medium|hard"
Restrict to one difficulty.
.TP
.BI \-\-days " N"
Look back N days.
.B 0
means all time.
.TP
.BI \-\-by " pattern|difficulty|tag|strategy"
Also print one distribution per group, ordered by volume.
.TP
.BI \-\-type " TYPE"
Which problem type. Defaults to the active list's. A distribution is only ever
over one: solve times for different kinds of problem do not share a scale.
.TP
.BI \-\-limit " N"
Maximum number of groups to print with
.BR \-\-by .
.TP
.BI \-\-min\-samples " N"
Sample count below which the 99th percentile is greyed out.
.SS queue
.TP
.BI \-n " N"
Problems in the queue. Defaults to
.I session.queue_n
from the configuration, which itself falls back to
.I session.planned_n
until you set it.
.TP
.B \-\-regenerate
Rebuild today's queue from the current cards, replacing the one already stored
for today.
.TP
.BI \-\-list " NAME"
Which list's queue. Defaults to
.IR session.active_list .
Every list has its own queue for the day, sized by
.I queue_n
on its set where the set gives one.
.SS history
.TP
.BI \-\-limit " N"
Show only the most recent N runs.
.TP
.BI \-\-run " N"
Expand one run, by run number, into its per-problem breakdown.
.SS seed
.TP
.BI \-\-file " PATH"
Seed one JSON list by hand, outside the configuration. Relative to the current
directory.
.TP
.BI \-\-list " NAME"
One set rather than all of them. With
.BR \-\-file ,
the name to seed it under.
.TP
.BI \-\-type " TYPE"
With
.BR \-\-file ,
the problem type its entries are.
.SS types
.TP
.BI \-\-new " NAME"
Write a type into
.I ~/.config/@SLUG@/types/
to edit. It starts as a copy of an existing type, comments included. A file
there takes precedence over a bundled type of the same name. Refuses to
overwrite one that already exists.
.TP
.BI \-\-from " TYPE"
The type
.B \-\-new
starts as a copy of. Defaults to the bundled type of the same name, where there
is one.
.SS fetch
.TP
.B \-\-refresh
Re-download problems already cached.
.TP
.BI \-\-list " NAME"
Cache this list instead of the active one.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-mb " N"
Cache size ceiling, overriding
.IR cache.max_mb .
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Print the priority order and what is already cached. Fetches nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-session
Prompt for your
.B LEETCODE_SESSION
cookie and store it at
.I ~/.config/@SLUG@/session
with mode 0600, then continue. Prompted rather than taken as an argument so it
never appears in
.BR ps (1)
or your shell history.
.SH KEYS
Navigation is vim's, on every screen. The arrow keys, Home/End and
PageUp/PageDown work everywhere too.
.TP
.BR j ", " k
Down, up.
.TP
.BR h ", " l
Left, right. Where a screen has two panes, these move between them; on the
queue and setup screens they step across the problem lists.
.TP
.BR gg ", " G
Top, bottom.
.TP
.BR ^d ", " ^u
Half a screen down, up.
.TP
.BR ^f ", " ^b
A full screen down, up.
.PP
Because those keys are motions everywhere, no screen binds them to an action.
.PP
On the home screen:
.B n
starts a run,
.B c
picks up a suspended one where you left it,
.B d
opens today's queue,
.B r
opens the runs,
.B t
opens the statistics,
.B s
opens the settings,
.B a
opens the methods screen,
.B p
opens the patterns screen,
.B q
quits. The menu is also a list, drawn in two columns, so
.BR j / k
move within a column,
.BR h / l
step across, and Enter reaches the same places. A suspended run gets a
full-width row of its own above the columns, because its label says where you
were rather than naming a screen. Everything you have mastered is listed under
the menu on the same screen \(em
.B m
moves the cursor into that list and Esc gives it back to the menu. Warming the offline cache is not on this
menu: it is a row on the settings screen, directly under the
.I cache.offline
switch it serves.
.PP
On the queue screen every row arrives checked.
.B Space
unchecks the one under the cursor,
.B ^e
and
.B ^x
check everything and nothing,
.B Enter
(or
.BR ^s )
starts a run over whatever is checked,
.B ^r
rebuilds the queue from the current cards, and
.B q
goes back. Unchecking a row shortens the run and leaves today's queue as
generated \(em what you skipped is still owed.
.BR h " and " l
step to the next list's queue: each problem set has its own for the day. That
changes what is on screen and nothing else;
.I session.active_list
is still the list the app opens on.
.PP
On the setup screen:
.B /
(or
.BR i )
moves to the filter box and
.B Escape
comes back out of it to the list — a second
.B Escape
leaves the screen.
.B c
moves to the count box, which is how many
.B ^r
rolls \(em for this run only, never written back to the configuration.
.B Space
picks a problem,
.B ^e
and
.B ^x
pick everything and nothing,
.B ^r
rolls a fresh random set,
.B ^a
turns speech mode on or off for this run only, and
.B ^s
starts the run.
.BR h " and " l
step across the problem lists, from the list and not from either text box. What
you have picked comes with you, so one run can hold problems of two types.
.PP
On the active problem screen:
.TP
.B o
Open the problem in a browser.
.TP
.B p
Pause and resume. Paused time is recorded, not hidden.
.TP
.B c
Show and hide the problem's pattern and tags. Free, and nothing is logged —
the point is that you have to decide to look.
.TP
.B r
Show and hide your own past attempts at this problem: how long ago each one
was, how long it took, how it ended and what it scored. Never the archived
code or the reflection note — those are on the history screen, where they
cannot spoil the problem in front of you. The line above the clock always
carries the most recent one.
.TP
.B ?
Reveal the next hint tier. Hints are monotonic and cannot be taken back; the
last tier is the full solution and ends the attempt.
.TP
.B s
Log a failed submit, then hand you an editor buffer to paste the code that was
rejected. Skippable with
.BR :q! ,
like every capture step, and switchable off in the settings screen. Offered only
for a problem type that has a judge to submit to.
.TP
.B f
Finish: how much help you needed, whether you could reconstruct it cold in a
month, and then whatever the problem's type asks. What follows describes a
LeetCode problem; see
.BR "@COMMAND@ types" .
The time and space complexities you claim with a separate
.I "was it optimal?"
answer for each, a
.I "code style"
answer saying whether you would have handed the code in, and the LeetCode
percentiles if you have them — then the
patterns that can solve the problem, then the problem's methods, then the two
editor steps. None of it is scored; the run
score stays a function of measured facts. The time optimality answer is the one
that moves a review, because a solution with the wrong asymptotics has not
learned the pattern the schedule exists for. Code style moves nothing: it is
recorded and shown back, and it answers in its own words
.RB ( clean ", " rough ", " "not sure" )
rather than the optimality ones, because nothing beats how code reads the way a
better complexity beats a worse one. Afterwards it offers you the same
problem again, with a fresh clock:
.B y
reruns it and
.B n
moves on. A rerun is recorded under the same attempt — its own verdict, time,
code file and note beside the first pass rather than over it — and it neither
scores anything nor moves a review, because the card was scheduled by the first
pass and one attempt is one review.
.TP
.B space
On the patterns prompt: tick a reusable pattern that can solve this problem. The
prompt opens with every pattern the problem is already tagged with ticked, and
they stay ticked whichever route you took tonight \(em the tags belong to the
problem, so the list answers "every problem quickselect is an answer to" rather
than "the evenings I reached for it". Ticking one off takes it off the problem,
and saving with nothing ticked leaves the problem untagged. On the methods
prompt that follows it: mark the method you wrote tonight, which is also what
tags the file you archive next.
.B i
names a new one on either; both lists are yours and both start empty. They are
separate lists and nothing joins them \(em a strategy means the same thing on
every problem, a method is one route through this one and is usually built out
of several patterns, which it says in its own name.
.B o
on the methods prompt cycles what a method costs: optimal, not optimal, not
sure, unclaimed. An optimal method recorded here that is not the one you wrote
keeps the grade a beaten solve would otherwise lose, and it can be recorded
months later. The prompt reads back what it derived about the solution, with the
answers that produced it. Saving the methods prompt with nothing picked records
nothing; saving the patterns prompt always records the list, because an empty
list is an answer about the problem.
.B esc
steps back one screen at a time, each reopening holding every answer you gave
it — nothing is written until all three prompts are done, so the attempt is
still running behind them. Switchable off in the settings screen.
.TP
.B x
Give up. Scores zero, and is still recorded. You are offered another pass at the
problem afterwards, which is where a second go is worth the most.
.TP
.B z
Suspend the run. The problem on screen keeps its clock, its hint tier and its
submits, and keeps no verdict, so nothing is scored and no review is scheduled
for a problem you are still in the middle of. Not available during a rerun,
whose clock would be written over the first pass's: finish it or drop it.
.B c
on the home screen hands it back, with the clock stopped until
.B p
starts it. Quitting hard does the same thing rather than recording a
.I gave_up
you never meant. Time spent away is kept separately from paused time.
.TP
.B q
End the run. With a problem still open it asks how:
.B y
records that problem as
.I gave_up
and opens the editor for the partial code, and
.B x
throws it away instead — nothing about it is recorded, no review is scheduled,
and no editor opens. Problems already finished in the run keep their scores
either way.
.SH PROBLEM TYPES AND SETS
A set is named in the configuration, under a table of its own:
.PP
.RS
.nf
[sets.system-design]
type = "system-design"
path = "sets/system-design.json"
queue_n = 1
.fi
.RE
.PP
.I type
is a bundled type or one in
.IR ~/.config/@SLUG@/types/ .
.I path
is a JSON list of problems, relative to the configuration file; leave it out to
use a list bundled with
.BR @NAME@ .
.I queue_n
and
.I reviews_per_day
are optional, and size this set's queue on their own. In the list only
.I title
is required of an entry;
.IR slug ,
.IR url ,
.IR difficulty ,
.IR pattern " and"
.I tags
are the rest. A
.I url
with no scheme is a file, relative to the list. The set is read the next time
the app opens.
.PP
To add a new kind of problem, write its type first:
.PP
.RS
.nf
@COMMAND@ types \-\-new distributed\-systems \-\-from system\-design
@COMMAND@ types distributed\-systems
.fi
.RE
.PP
The first writes a copy of an existing type to edit. The second reads it back:
every field, what its answer does to a review, and anything in the file that
could not be made sense of. Then write the list, name the set, and run
.B @COMMAND@ seed
to see what came in.
.PP
A type's fields are
.IR text ", " number ", " fraction
(a share such as 7/10, stored as typed) or
.I choice
(one answer off a ladder). A field may carry a
.I review
rule saying which answers fail an attempt, which demote it to Hard, and which
an Easy grade requires. Answers move reviews and never the score. The type is
read when a card is graded rather than copied into the log, so changing a rule
and running
.B @COMMAND@ replay
regrades every attempt already logged under it.
.PP
Two sets that claim one slug share the problem if they are the same type, and
collide if they are not: the first keeps it and the second's entry is left out
and named. Nothing about a type or a set can stop a run. A list that will not
load costs that list, a field that makes no sense is dropped, and
.B @COMMAND@ doctor
says what was wrong.
.PP
Each set has its own queue for the day. On the queue and setup screens
.BR h " and " l
step across the lists.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/@SLUG@/config.toml
Written on first run with every default present and commented. The settings
screen never rewrites it: changes made there are events layered on top, and
.B x
on a settings row drops the override and takes this file's value back.
.TP
.I ~/.config/@SLUG@/types/<type>.toml
Problem types you have written or changed, one file each. Optional: two types
are bundled, and a file here takes precedence over a bundled type of the same
name. Read when it is needed rather than copied into the log, so editing one and
running
.B replay
regrades every review under the new rules.
.TP
.I ~/.config/@SLUG@/session
Your LeetCode session cookie, if you have one, mode 0600. Optional: without it
premium problems cannot be cached. Kept out of both
.I config.toml
and the settings table on purpose — the settings table is a projection of an
append-only log, so a credential written there could never be removed and would
survive every replay.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/@SLUG@/@SLUG@.db
The event log and the projections built from it.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/@SLUG@/cache/<problem>.html
Cached problem statements, self-contained. Derived and disposable: deleting the
directory costs nothing but a re-fetch.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/@SLUG@/code/<problem>/<attempt-id>.<ext>
Archived solutions.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/@SLUG@/code/<problem>/<attempt-id>-wrong<n>.<ext>
Archived failed submits, beside the solution they became.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/@SLUG@/code/<problem>/method-<method>.<ext>
A method written from the methods screen, with no attempt behind it.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/@SLUG@/notes/<problem>/<attempt-id>.md
Reflection notes.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/@SLUG@/audio/<problem>/<attempt-id>.opus
Speech-mode recordings, one per attempt. Only present when speech mode was on
for the run.
.PP
Solutions, notes and recordings are kept as ordinary files rather than in the
database so they can be searched, diffed and opened with the usual tools.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B @ENVPFX@_HOME
Relocate the configuration and data directories under one path. Useful for a
throwaway profile.
.TP
.B @ENVPFX@_DB
Use a specific database file.
.TP
.B @ENVPFX@_EDITOR
Editor for the capture steps. Takes precedence over
.B VISUAL
and
.BR EDITOR .
.TP
.B @ENVPFX@_BROWSER
Command used to open a problem.
.TP
.B @ENVPFX@_FFMPEG
The recorder spawned by speech mode. Defaults to
.B ffmpeg
on the path; without one, runs simply go unrecorded.
.TP
.B @ENVPFX@_LEETCODE_SESSION
Your
.B LEETCODE_SESSION
cookie, unlocking premium problems for
.BR fetch .
Takes precedence over the
.I session
file. Never printed, never logged, and never written into a cached page.
.TP
.BR VISUAL ", " EDITOR
Consulted, in that order, when
.B @ENVPFX@_EDITOR
is unset. Without any of them the capture steps are skipped, which costs
nothing but loses the notes.
.TP
.BR XDG_CONFIG_HOME ", " XDG_DATA_HOME
Respected when
.B @ENVPFX@_HOME
is unset.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 2
The command line could not be parsed.
.SH NOTES
Notes are collected from the first run even though nothing reads them yet.
Measurements can be recomputed from the event log forever; reflections cannot
be written after the fact.
